Optimized Approach (Sliding Window on s+s)
Concatenate s+s.

Slide a window of length n.

Each window = one rotation.

Count score with two pointers.

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int countScore(const string &s) {
    int n = s.size();
    int score = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        if (s[i] == s[i + 1]) score++;
    }
    return score;
}

int optimized(string s, int k) {
    int n = s.size();
    string doubled = s + s;
    int ans = 0;
    for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {
        string window = doubled.substr(start, n);
        if (countScore(window) == k) ans++;
    }
    return ans;
}

int main() {
    string s = "abca";
    int k = 0;
    cout << optimized(s, k) << endl; // Output: 1
}

automatic : shortcut to find prefix and suffix: string rotated = s.substr(rot) + s.substr(0, rot);
string rotate(string s, int rot) {
    string result = "";
    for (int i = rot; i < s.size(); i++) result += s[i]; // suffix
    for (int i = 0; i < rot; i++) result += s[i];        // prefix
    return result;
}

🔎 Prefix + Suffix vs Sliding Window (Techniques used to generate all possible rotation)
Prefix + Suffix (brute)

You literally rebuild each rotation:
rotated = nums[rot..n-1] + nums[0..rot-1]

For each rotation, you create a new array/string.

Time Complexity:

Building one rotation = O(n).

Doing it for all n rotations = O(n²).

Space Complexity: O(n) per rotation (since you copy).

Sliding Window

Trick: double the array → nums + nums.

Every rotation is just a window of size n.

You don’t rebuild from scratch, you just “slide” the window.

Time Complexity:

Extracting each window = O(n).

Doing it for all n rotations = still O(n²).

But with prefix sums, you can compute half sums in O(1), reducing total to O(n).

Space Complexity: O(2n) for doubled array.

✅ Key Insight
Prefix + Suffix is the brute force style because you explicitly rebuild each rotation.

Sliding Window is the optimized style because it avoids manual rebuilding and can be combined with prefix sums to cut down repeated sum calculations.

So yes — it’s based on time complexity and efficiency:

Prefix + Suffix = brute force (O(n²)).

Sliding Window (with prefix sums) = optimized (O(n)).

